1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,250
On a Saturday night in July 1945,

2
00:00:03,270 --> 00:00:05,546
in a small farm town in Utah,

3
00:00:05,566 --> 00:00:08,498
an American soldier sat in a cafe on Main

4
00:00:08,518 --> 00:00:10,794
Street and had a cup of coffee.

5
00:00:10,814 --> 00:00:12,434
He was twenty-three years old,

6
00:00:12,454 --> 00:00:13,090
and slight.

7
00:00:13,110 --> 00:00:15,714
Before he left, he told the waitress that

8
00:00:15,734 --> 00:00:18,030
something exciting was going to happen tonight.

9
00:00:19,325 --> 00:00:22,333
He walked a few blocks to the east end of

10
00:00:22,353 --> 00:00:24,755
Main Street, to a camp behind barbed wire,

11
00:00:24,775 --> 00:00:26,875
where about two hundred and fifty German

12
00:00:26,895 --> 00:00:28,994
prisoners of war were asleep in tents.

13
00:00:29,014 --> 00:00:31,417
The war in Europe had been over for

14
00:00:31,437 --> 00:00:32,345
exactly two months.

15
00:00:33,650 --> 00:00:36,672
At midnight he reported for guard duty.

16
00:00:36,692 --> 00:00:38,845
He climbed a guard tower.

17
00:00:38,865 --> 00:00:41,888
There was a machine gun up there.

18
00:00:41,908 --> 00:00:43,626
It was not loaded.

19
00:00:43,646 --> 00:00:44,950
He loaded it.

20
00:00:46,125 --> 00:00:48,081
This is the documented story of what

21
00:00:48,101 --> 00:00:50,479
happened at Salina, Utah, of the fifteen

22
00:00:50,499 --> 00:00:52,430
seconds that killed nine men who were

23
00:00:52,450 --> 00:00:54,659
waiting to go home, and of the American

24
00:00:54,679 --> 00:00:56,609
who pulled the trigger, and was never

25
00:00:56,629 --> 00:00:57,465
tried for it.

26
00:00:59,050 --> 00:01:02,325
Salina was a farm town in the middle of

27
00:01:02,345 --> 00:01:04,022
Utah, in sugar beet country.

28
00:01:04,042 --> 00:01:06,738
With so many men away at the war,

29
00:01:06,758 --> 00:01:09,792
the beets still had to be thinned and harvested.

30
00:01:09,812 --> 00:01:11,170
The farmers needed hands.

31
00:01:12,425 --> 00:01:14,154
The Army had the hands.

32
00:01:14,174 --> 00:01:16,252
More than four hundred thousand prisoners

33
00:01:16,272 --> 00:01:18,351
of war were held in America,

34
00:01:18,371 --> 00:01:20,799
and many of them worked on farms.

35
00:01:20,819 --> 00:01:22,898
Utah alone held thousands of them,

36
00:01:22,918 --> 00:01:25,346
Germans and Italians, and many of the

37
00:01:25,366 --> 00:01:28,700
Germans had been captured with the Afrika Korps.

38
00:01:29,400 --> 00:01:31,400
On the eighth of June, 1945,

39
00:01:31,420 --> 00:01:34,000
a new branch camp opened in Salina,

40
00:01:34,020 --> 00:01:36,415
in an old Civilian Conservation Corps camp

41
00:01:36,435 --> 00:01:38,485
at the end of Main Street.

42
00:01:38,505 --> 00:01:40,555
It had forty-three tents with wooden

43
00:01:40,575 --> 00:01:42,970
floors, about six men to a tent,

44
00:01:42,990 --> 00:01:46,075
a high barbed wire fence, and three guard towers.

45
00:01:46,095 --> 00:01:49,180
It was strange for a prison camp to sit

46
00:01:49,200 --> 00:01:50,215
inside a town.

47
00:01:50,235 --> 00:01:53,340
Children lived a few hundred feet from the wire.

48
00:01:54,650 --> 00:01:57,666
The prisoners were sent out to the beet

49
00:01:57,686 --> 00:01:58,804
fields every day.

50
00:01:58,824 --> 00:02:02,219
A prisoner was paid eighty cents a day in

51
00:02:02,239 --> 00:02:02,978
camp coupons.

52
00:02:02,998 --> 00:02:05,254
Germany had surrendered a month before

53
00:02:05,274 --> 00:02:06,013
they arrived.

54
00:02:06,033 --> 00:02:08,310
They were waiting for a ship.

55
00:02:09,975 --> 00:02:12,754
The men who guarded them were the men the

56
00:02:12,774 --> 00:02:13,687
Army could spare.

57
00:02:13,707 --> 00:02:15,865
They were soldiers judged unfit for the

58
00:02:15,885 --> 00:02:17,420
front, because of their health,

59
00:02:17,440 --> 00:02:18,995
or because of their records.

60
00:02:20,400 --> 00:02:23,163
Clarence Bertucci was born in New Orleans

61
00:02:23,183 --> 00:02:25,480
in 1921.

62
00:02:25,500 --> 00:02:27,961
He had seven brothers and six sisters.

63
00:02:27,981 --> 00:02:30,442
He left school after sixth grade and

64
00:02:30,462 --> 00:02:33,840
joined the Army in 1940.

65
00:02:33,860 --> 00:02:35,835
Two of his brothers were serving overseas,

66
00:02:35,855 --> 00:02:37,280
one of them in Germany.

67
00:02:38,675 --> 00:02:41,355
He spent eight months in England with an

68
00:02:41,375 --> 00:02:43,381
artillery unit, and never saw combat.

69
00:02:43,401 --> 00:02:46,419
After five years in the Army he was still

70
00:02:46,439 --> 00:02:47,094
a private.

71
00:02:47,114 --> 00:02:49,119
He had gone absent without leave,

72
00:02:49,139 --> 00:02:50,807
he had refused guard duty,

73
00:02:50,827 --> 00:02:52,495
he had missed a train.

74
00:02:52,515 --> 00:02:55,215
He had been in the hospital twelve times.

75
00:02:56,500 --> 00:02:59,443
People in Salina said later that he talked

76
00:02:59,463 --> 00:03:01,265
about getting his Germans someday.

77
00:03:01,285 --> 00:03:04,181
Whether he really said it cannot be proved.

78
00:03:04,201 --> 00:03:07,097
The Army's own record of his words came

79
00:03:07,117 --> 00:03:08,940
afterwards, and it was worse.

80
00:03:10,650 --> 00:03:12,383
July seventh was a Saturday.

81
00:03:12,403 --> 00:03:15,189
The prisoners spent the whole day in the

82
00:03:15,209 --> 00:03:17,293
beet fields, came back, ate dinner,

83
00:03:17,313 --> 00:03:19,046
and went to their tents.

84
00:03:19,066 --> 00:03:21,170
By midnight the camp was asleep.

85
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:25,240
The machine guns in the towers were kept

86
00:03:25,260 --> 00:03:28,000
empty, with the ammunition in boxes beside them.

87
00:03:28,020 --> 00:03:30,808
Bertucci took a belt of two hundred and

88
00:03:30,828 --> 00:03:32,516
fifty rounds, fed it into the gun,

89
00:03:32,536 --> 00:03:34,000
and turned it on the tents.

90
00:03:35,325 --> 00:03:37,999
Shortly after midnight, he pulled the trigger.

91
00:03:38,019 --> 00:03:41,079
He fired three bursts, moving the gun from

92
00:03:41,099 --> 00:03:43,003
left to right, and back.

93
00:03:43,023 --> 00:03:44,928
It took about fifteen seconds.

94
00:03:44,948 --> 00:03:47,237
The bullets went through the canvas,

95
00:03:47,257 --> 00:03:50,316
through the beds, and through the wooden floors.

96
00:03:50,336 --> 00:03:53,011
Thirty of the forty-three tents were hit.

97
00:03:53,031 --> 00:03:55,725
In one tent alone, four men died.

98
00:03:57,050 --> 00:03:58,847
The camp commander, Lieutenant Albert

99
00:03:58,867 --> 00:04:02,481
Cornell, was lying on a cot at the foot of

100
00:04:02,501 --> 00:04:03,571
the same tower.

101
00:04:03,591 --> 00:04:06,115
He ran outside and saw no escape,

102
00:04:06,135 --> 00:04:08,296
no fight, no reason at all.

103
00:04:08,316 --> 00:04:10,839
He shouted at the guard to stop.

104
00:04:10,859 --> 00:04:12,656
The belt was already empty.

105
00:04:12,676 --> 00:04:15,855
Up in the tower, Bertucci was calling for

106
00:04:15,875 --> 00:04:16,552
more ammunition.

107
00:04:16,572 --> 00:04:19,010
He said he was not through yet.

108
00:04:20,250 --> 00:04:22,642
A corporal climbed the tower and brought

109
00:04:22,662 --> 00:04:23,332
him down.

110
00:04:23,352 --> 00:04:24,730
He did not resist.

111
00:04:26,475 --> 00:04:30,235
The noise woke nearly everyone in Salina.

112
00:04:30,255 --> 00:04:32,141
Many people thought the Germans were

113
00:04:32,161 --> 00:04:32,777
breaking out.

114
00:04:32,797 --> 00:04:35,636
A boy of six, who lived three hundred feet

115
00:04:35,656 --> 00:04:38,813
away, went out on the front porch with his family.

116
00:04:38,833 --> 00:04:41,375
They could hear the cries of the prisoners.

117
00:04:42,750 --> 00:04:44,730
The town's doctor, Rae Noyes,

118
00:04:44,750 --> 00:04:47,852
reached the camp and called it a horrible mess.

119
00:04:47,872 --> 00:04:49,934
Guards and prisoners were already working

120
00:04:49,954 --> 00:04:51,322
on the wounded together.

121
00:04:51,342 --> 00:04:54,250
The men were carried to the little Salina

122
00:04:54,270 --> 00:04:55,630
hospital on their cots.

123
00:04:56,900 --> 00:04:58,667
It filled up at once.

124
00:04:58,687 --> 00:05:01,526
The rooms, the hall, and the waiting room

125
00:05:01,546 --> 00:05:04,385
were full, and the rest were treated outside.

126
00:05:04,405 --> 00:05:05,814
Nurses and townspeople helped.

127
00:05:05,834 --> 00:05:09,030
One man, whose body had been almost cut in

128
00:05:09,050 --> 00:05:11,532
half, lived until six in the morning.

129
00:05:11,552 --> 00:05:15,105
The dead were laid out on the west lawn of

130
00:05:15,125 --> 00:05:15,925
the hospital.

131
00:05:17,150 --> 00:05:19,726
Six men had died in the camp.

132
00:05:19,746 --> 00:05:22,630
Two more died in Salina.

133
00:05:22,650 --> 00:05:25,583
The others were taken to Army hospitals in

134
00:05:25,603 --> 00:05:26,322
the north.

135
00:05:26,342 --> 00:05:28,906
One was expected to lose a foot.

136
00:05:28,926 --> 00:05:31,510
Another had been hit in the eye.

137
00:05:32,725 --> 00:05:34,688
These men had survived the war.

138
00:05:34,708 --> 00:05:37,332
They died in a beet town in Utah,

139
00:05:37,352 --> 00:05:39,005
two months after the peace.

140
00:05:40,700 --> 00:05:43,744
The Army sent a board of officers from

141
00:05:43,764 --> 00:05:45,659
Ogden, under Colonel Arthur Ericsson.

142
00:05:45,679 --> 00:05:47,957
The prisoners' spokesman, a sergeant named

143
00:05:47,977 --> 00:05:50,150
Hans Fertig, testified before it.

144
00:05:50,170 --> 00:05:52,508
He told them the guards had never

145
00:05:52,528 --> 00:05:54,865
threatened anyone, and had never fired a

146
00:05:54,885 --> 00:05:55,538
shot before.

147
00:05:55,558 --> 00:05:57,559
But the machine guns, he said,

148
00:05:57,579 --> 00:05:59,916
had always made the men afraid that

149
00:05:59,936 --> 00:06:01,620
something like this might happen.

150
00:06:02,900 --> 00:06:04,837
Fertig asked for one thing.

151
00:06:04,857 --> 00:06:07,591
He asked that the prisoners be given two

152
00:06:07,611 --> 00:06:08,411
days off.

153
00:06:09,500 --> 00:06:11,660
The colonel told him it was highly

154
00:06:11,680 --> 00:06:14,463
desirable that the men do their work as usual.

155
00:06:14,483 --> 00:06:16,643
On Monday morning, with their friends dead

156
00:06:16,663 --> 00:06:18,823
or in the hospital, the prisoners went

157
00:06:18,843 --> 00:06:20,400
back to the beet fields.

158
00:06:21,750 --> 00:06:24,038
They questioned Bertucci at length.

159
00:06:24,058 --> 00:06:25,413
He showed no remorse.

160
00:06:25,433 --> 00:06:28,509
He said he had often been tempted to turn

161
00:06:28,529 --> 00:06:31,948
the gun on them, and that he was not at

162
00:06:31,968 --> 00:06:32,635
all sorry.

163
00:06:32,655 --> 00:06:35,387
Pressed for a reason, he gave only one.

164
00:06:35,407 --> 00:06:37,470
He just did not like Germans.

165
00:06:38,825 --> 00:06:40,981
Time magazine put it this way.

166
00:06:41,001 --> 00:06:44,265
He had hated Germans, so he had killed Germans.

167
00:06:46,025 --> 00:06:48,420
The front page that reported those words

168
00:06:48,440 --> 00:06:50,490
on July tenth carried another story,

169
00:06:50,510 --> 00:06:51,545
right beside them.

170
00:06:52,775 --> 00:06:55,058
That morning, at Fort Leavenworth in

171
00:06:55,078 --> 00:06:57,744
Kansas, the United States Army hanged five

172
00:06:57,764 --> 00:06:59,279
German prisoners of war.

173
00:06:59,299 --> 00:07:02,350
They had murdered a fellow prisoner in a

174
00:07:02,370 --> 00:07:03,501
camp in Oklahoma.

175
00:07:03,521 --> 00:07:06,572
It was the first time enemy prisoners had

176
00:07:06,592 --> 00:07:08,107
been executed in America.

177
00:07:08,127 --> 00:07:10,409
In the Ogden paper that day,

178
00:07:10,429 --> 00:07:13,096
the five hanged Germans and the American

179
00:07:13,116 --> 00:07:15,035
guard ran side by side.

180
00:07:16,300 --> 00:07:19,100
The Salt Lake Tribune called the shooting

181
00:07:19,120 --> 00:07:21,114
not only deplorable but damnable.

182
00:07:21,134 --> 00:07:23,970
It said Bertucci had disgraced the army.

183
00:07:23,990 --> 00:07:26,542
But some telegrams reached the Army that

184
00:07:26,562 --> 00:07:28,400
summer in support of him.

185
00:07:30,075 --> 00:07:31,620
On Thursday, July twelfth,

186
00:07:31,640 --> 00:07:34,751
the eight dead men were buried at Fort

187
00:07:34,771 --> 00:07:36,708
Douglas, in Salt Lake City.

188
00:07:36,728 --> 00:07:38,274
They wore American khaki.

189
00:07:38,294 --> 00:07:40,622
An American chaplain read the service,

190
00:07:40,642 --> 00:07:43,361
and a firing squad of American soldiers

191
00:07:43,381 --> 00:07:45,318
gave them a military salute,

192
00:07:45,338 --> 00:07:47,295
as the Geneva Convention required.

193
00:07:48,675 --> 00:07:50,685
The public was not allowed.

194
00:07:50,705 --> 00:07:53,420
About fifteen men from the Salina camp

195
00:07:53,440 --> 00:07:56,111
were brought north under guard to see

196
00:07:56,131 --> 00:07:57,265
their friends buried.

197
00:07:57,285 --> 00:07:59,956
A choir of German prisoners from Ogden

198
00:07:59,976 --> 00:08:01,879
sang the old soldiers' song,

199
00:08:01,899 --> 00:08:03,032
The Good Comrade.

200
00:08:03,052 --> 00:08:04,975
Then a bugler played Taps.

201
00:08:06,275 --> 00:08:09,344
The next day, in the hospital at Kearns,

202
00:08:09,364 --> 00:08:10,889
the ninth man died.

203
00:08:10,909 --> 00:08:12,820
Friedrich Ritter was forty-eight years

204
00:08:12,840 --> 00:08:13,978
old, and married.

205
00:08:13,998 --> 00:08:16,315
He was buried beside the others.

206
00:08:17,625 --> 00:08:20,224
The rest of the dead were young,

207
00:08:20,244 --> 00:08:22,468
most of them in their twenties.

208
00:08:22,488 --> 00:08:24,338
Two more had wives waiting.

209
00:08:24,358 --> 00:08:26,957
One history says their families were not

210
00:08:26,977 --> 00:08:29,300
told until 1948.

211
00:08:30,400 --> 00:08:32,773
An Army captain from the Ogden camp,

212
00:08:32,793 --> 00:08:34,825
Wayne Owens, was given the case.

213
00:08:34,845 --> 00:08:37,218
He looked at the evidence and concluded

214
00:08:37,238 --> 00:08:41,012
that Bertucci was sane, and that this was

215
00:08:41,032 --> 00:08:42,487
a calculated act.

216
00:08:42,507 --> 00:08:44,475
He recommended a court-martial.

217
00:08:45,175 --> 00:08:48,108
His superiors told him he had no authority

218
00:08:48,128 --> 00:08:49,215
to decide that.

219
00:08:49,235 --> 00:08:52,491
Bertucci told officers he had planned it

220
00:08:52,511 --> 00:08:53,216
in advance.

221
00:08:53,236 --> 00:08:55,030
He had told a waitress.

222
00:08:55,050 --> 00:08:58,294
He had waited for the other guards to go

223
00:08:58,314 --> 00:08:59,019
to bed.

224
00:08:59,039 --> 00:09:01,215
He had loaded the gun himself.

225
00:09:02,550 --> 00:09:05,076
Doctors at Bushnell Hospital in Brigham

226
00:09:05,096 --> 00:09:07,621
City observed him for several weeks.

227
00:09:07,641 --> 00:09:11,495
In August 1945, they found him mentally

228
00:09:11,515 --> 00:09:11,887
unbalanced.

229
00:09:11,907 --> 00:09:13,452
There was no court-martial,

230
00:09:13,472 --> 00:09:14,626
then or ever.

231
00:09:14,646 --> 00:09:18,147
He was sent to an Army mental hospital on

232
00:09:18,167 --> 00:09:18,967
Long Island.

233
00:09:20,275 --> 00:09:23,055
He spent much of the rest of his life in

234
00:09:23,075 --> 00:09:26,615
mental hospitals, and died in 1969.

235
00:09:26,635 --> 00:09:29,925
Nobody was ever tried for the deaths at Salina.

236
00:09:31,025 --> 00:09:33,601
The wounded recovered and were sent back

237
00:09:33,621 --> 00:09:34,343
to Germany.

238
00:09:34,363 --> 00:09:36,568
A later agreement between the two

239
00:09:36,588 --> 00:09:38,793
countries meant they could not claim

240
00:09:38,813 --> 00:09:40,647
compensation from the United States.

241
00:09:40,667 --> 00:09:43,243
Their only help would be a German

242
00:09:43,263 --> 00:09:44,063
veteran's pension.

243
00:09:45,400 --> 00:09:49,500
The camp emptied by the end of 1945.

244
00:09:49,520 --> 00:09:51,224
The next spring, German prisoners were

245
00:09:51,244 --> 00:09:54,280
back in the fields around Salina.

246
00:09:54,300 --> 00:09:57,251
Later, farm workers lived on the site.

247
00:09:57,271 --> 00:10:00,053
Decades on, Salina's own mayor did not

248
00:10:00,073 --> 00:10:02,060
learn the story until after he was elected.

249
00:10:03,425 --> 00:10:06,810
In 1977, a man named Dieter Lampe came

250
00:10:06,830 --> 00:10:08,745
back to Salina.

251
00:10:08,765 --> 00:10:11,392
He had been shot in the arm that night.

252
00:10:11,412 --> 00:10:13,450
He said he had received excellent care,

253
00:10:13,470 --> 00:10:15,803
and that the war had left him strongly

254
00:10:15,823 --> 00:10:16,705
opposed to war.

255
00:10:17,950 --> 00:10:21,686
In 1988, the German Air Force paid to

256
00:10:21,706 --> 00:10:24,272
restore the memorial statue at Fort Douglas.

257
00:10:24,292 --> 00:10:27,691
Two men wounded in 1945 stood in the

258
00:10:27,711 --> 00:10:29,079
cemetery for the ceremony.

259
00:10:29,099 --> 00:10:32,202
In 2016, the family of a farmer who had

260
00:10:32,222 --> 00:10:34,631
hired prisoners opened a small museum on

261
00:10:34,651 --> 00:10:35,325
the site.

262
00:10:35,345 --> 00:10:37,060
They keep tent pegs there,

263
00:10:37,080 --> 00:10:40,183
and a jewelry box a prisoner made out of

264
00:10:40,203 --> 00:10:41,003
matchsticks.

265
00:10:41,800 --> 00:10:43,874
If your family remembers the prisoners in

266
00:10:43,894 --> 00:10:46,266
the beet fields of Utah, or anywhere else

267
00:10:46,286 --> 00:10:48,380
in America, tell us in the comments.

268
00:10:50,150 --> 00:10:52,497
In the back corner of the cemetery at Fort

269
00:10:52,517 --> 00:10:54,601
Douglas, the nine men lie where the Army

270
00:10:54,621 --> 00:10:58,837
put them in 1945, a long way from the sea

271
00:10:58,857 --> 00:11:00,426
they were waiting to cross.

272
00:11:00,446 --> 00:11:02,014
They had outlived the war.

273
00:11:02,034 --> 00:11:04,874
They could not outlive one man who did not

274
00:11:04,894 --> 00:11:05,530
like Germans.

